const schema = [{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Service","name":"Post-Editing","serviceType":"Translation service","provider":{"@type":"Organization","name":"Pubrica","url":"https://pubrica.com/"},"url":"https://pubrica.com/services/editing-and-translation/post-editing/","description":"Human post-editing of machine and AI translation to ISO 18587. Full post-editing $0.06 a word, light $0.035. Free provenance triage before you buy.","areaServed":"Worldwide","audience":{"@type":"Audience","audienceType":"Researchers, PhD scholars, research institutions, medical writers and life-science teams"}},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"What is post-editing?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The human correction of machine-translated or AI-generated text, done against the source document, to a level agreed in advance. The international standard is ISO 18587:2017, which sets the requirements for the process and the competences of post-editors.\n\nThe against-the-source part is what makes it post-editing rather than editing. The errors that matter in machine output — a dropped negation, an omitted clause, a hedge that has become a finding, a unit converted at the wrong magnitude — all produce fluent, grammatical English and are undetectable to anyone reading only the target."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the difference between raw machine translation and post-edited translation?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A human reading the output against the source.\n\nRaw machine output is fluent, immediate and unverified. It ships with no human reviewer checking the output against the source before delivery, which is what makes it cheap and what makes it unusable for publication.\n\nPost-edited translation is the same output after somebody has compared every sentence with the source. The distance between $0.01 and $0.06 is the entire human contribution — which on the sample published here amounted to thirty-one meaning-level corrections, four of which changed what the paper reported."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Why is human post-editing needed after AI or machine translation?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Because machine output fails fluently, and fluency is what removes your ability to check it.\n\nNeural engines produce confident, grammatical target text that occasionally inverts a negation, drops a hedge, omits a clause without leaving a gap, or converts a unit wrongly. None of those looks like an error in English.\n\nLanguage models add a failure of their own: they improve on the source — smoothing an awkward sentence into a clearer claim than the author made, inserting a connective that asserts causation. In a research manuscript that is not a language error; it is a data-integrity problem. It is also why ISO/DIS 18587, balloting since 4 September 2026, is retitled for “non-human translation output”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the difference between full and light post-editing, and which do I need?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"ISO 18587 defines full post-editing as producing output “comparable to a product obtained by human translation” and light post-editing as producing “a merely comprehensible text”.\n\nAccuracy is in both. Light is not a weaker standard of correctness — meaning is right, nothing added or omitted — it is a decision to stop once the text is correct. Full adds terminology consistency, grammar and register to publication standard, and formatting.\n\nOn the sample published here that difference was $120: light would have delivered all thirty-one meaning corrections and left twenty-three terminology inconsistencies. Publishing it → full. Reading it to find something out → light. One caveat: light sits in informative Annex B, so nobody can be certified for it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"When is post-editing the wrong purchase?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"When the raw output is poor and the text must reach publication standard. Post-editing then costs more than translating from the source, and produces a worse result.\n\nTwo reasons. A post-editor working on bad output spends their time diagnosing what the machine did to the meaning, sentence by sentence against the source — the whole of translation plus the overhead of untangling wrong guesses. And heavily post-edited bad output keeps its original sentence architecture, so it reads as a translation permanently.\n\nA third case: output already edited monolingually by a colleague is hardest of all, because the editing removed the surface signals of machine origin without touching the meaning errors underneath. We price that above raw output."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do I have to tell the journal that I used a machine or an AI tool?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No uniform rule exists across publishers, and the safe course is to disclose.\n\nTaylor & Francis lists “copyediting, language improvement and translations” among supported uses and requires the tool’s name and version. Springer Nature places translation in its permitted tier, disclosure encouraged. Elsevier (July 2026) permits AI only for language and readability and requires a declaration, without naming translation. Wiley (July 2026) requires disclosure, exempts spelling and grammar tools, and is also silent on translation. The ICMJE’s January 2026 AI section does not mention translation either.\n\nTwo of the four address it explicitly, two are silent — silence is not permission. We write the declaration. No declaration transfers responsibility: an AI tool cannot be an author, so accuracy remains yours."}}]},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://pubrica.com/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Editing and translation","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/editing-and-translation/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Editing and Translation","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/editing-and-translation/post-editing/"}]}];

